+\begin{document}
+\title{EchoForge: A Strict-Open Synthetic Benchmark for Low-Altitude UAS Radar, Sensor Fusion, and Engineered Intelligence}
+\author{Jeppson Taylor, Lead Author\\NeverHuman Research Group}
+\maketitle
+
+\begin{abstract}
+EchoForge is a strict-open synthetic benchmark for low-altitude uncrewed aerial system radar and multimodal sensing research. The paper has one central experiment: compare accepted human-engineered radar/cue processing and prior fusion against a locked Engineered Intelligence (EI) sparse calibrated late-fusion candidate on the same blind, group-locked holdout. The benchmark run contains 10,000 scenario groups, 50 fixed-wing pusher-prop public-proxy positive groups, and 30,000 phase records. The positive class is inspired by public fixed-wing pusher-prop reporting but is not a measured Iranian-drone radar signature, not a fielded-sensor surrogate, and not an operational performance claim. The primary KPI is LCB95 Recall@$\leq$1\%FPR. On the 4,500-record blind holdout, with 24 positive records from eight positive groups, EI reaches point Recall@$\leq$1\%FPR of \kpigain{0.833 vs 0.292} (\kpigain{+54.1 pp}, \kpigain{+185\%}, \kpigain{2.85x}) and LCB95 Recall@$\leq$1\%FPR of \kpigain{0.699 vs 0.083} (\kpigain{+61.6 pp}, \kpigain{+742\%}, \kpigain{8.42x}) over accepted prior fusion. EI also raises AP from \kpigain{0.128} to \kpigain{0.825} and cuts selected-threshold false positives from \kpigain{49 to 1}. \kpicaveat{ROC AUC is lower for EI, 0.917 vs 0.938}, so the claim is improved low-FPR operating behavior under declared synthetic assumptions, not universal rank dominance or measured-platform truth.
+\end{abstract}
+
+\begin{IEEEkeywords}
+Synthetic radar, UAS detection, public proxy, micro-Doppler, sensor fusion, engineered intelligence, calibration, reproducibility.
+\end{IEEEkeywords}
+
+\section{What This Paper Tests}
+\textbf{Question.} If a reviewer gives every method the same synthetic radar/cue data, group-locked split, train/CV discovery path, and blind holdout, does a locked EI fusion candidate improve the low-FPR operating point over accepted prior fusion?
+
+\textbf{Answer.} Yes for this declared synthetic public-proxy run: EI improves LCB95 Recall@$\leq$1\%FPR from 0.083 to 0.699, while reducing selected-threshold false alarms from 49 to 1. The result is narrow and auditable: it is a benchmark result, not an operational sensor result.
+
