+\section{Best Practices for Synthetic Radar Simulation}
+The simulation follows a radar-first decomposition: target return, micro-Doppler, RCS/aspect, clutter/noise, receiver impairments, multipath/RFI, cue streams, and detector-view materialization. The target return is defined by public-proxy geometry, aspect-dependent RCS, Doppler centroid, and micro-Doppler cadence. Environment and sensor stressors are layered separately: thermal/SNR buckets, Weibull/K-like clutter, sea/terrain/weather stress, multipath ghosts, RFI bursts, AGC compression, clock drift, quantization, dropped CPI, PRF ambiguity, Doppler folding, and calibration offset.
+
+The received-power proxy is
+\begin{equation}
+P_r \propto \frac{P_tG_tG_r\lambda^2\sigma(\theta,f,\phi)}{(4\pi)^3R^4L}.
+\end{equation}
+The synthetic IQ record is
+\begin{equation}
+x_{m,n}=a_{m,n}e^{j(2\pi f_dmT_r+\varphi_n)}+c_{m,n}+\epsilon_{m,n}.
+\end{equation}
+The range-Doppler proxy uses
+\begin{equation}
+\Delta R=\frac{c}{2B},\qquad \Delta f_D=\frac{1}{T_{\mathrm{CPI}}},\qquad \Delta v=\frac{\lambda}{2T_{\mathrm{CPI}}}.
+\end{equation}
+These equations provide structure, not platform truth. The appendix records where every assumption enters the benchmark \cite{skolnik2008,richards2014,rohling1983,ward1990,kay1998,vantrees2002,chen2011,tahmoush2015}.
+
+\subsection{Scenario Phases}
+\textbf{Initial take-up, 0--30 s:} ground coupling, less settled kinematics, and clutter/multipath stress. \textbf{Climb transition, 30--90 s:} stronger kinematic separation but still changing aspect and handoff stress. \textbf{Cruise altitude, 90--150 s:} steadier geometry and the cleanest detector case. Phase conclusions remain diagnostic because each phase has only eight positive holdout records.
+
+\subsection{Positive Proxy and Hard Negatives}
+The public-proxy positive class uses a delta/swept fixed-wing pusher-prop geometry, 45--60 m/s speed envelope, pusher-prop micro-Doppler proxy of 150--217 Hz, and an aspect-dependent RCS envelope of -28 to -2 dBsm. Hard negatives include bird families, RC fixed-wing aircraft, weather cells, ground vehicles, wind turbines, terrain glint, multipath ghosts, RFI bursts, and clutter-only counterfactuals. The library is a robustness stressor, not an evasion model or population-rate model.
+
+\section{Detector Views and Accepted Processing}
+Detector-facing tables intentionally contain fewer fields than the generator state. X/Ku, S-band, and GBAD branches expose radar numeric summaries such as concentration, Doppler spread, CFAR/MTD-style scores, track confidence, horizon/revisit stress, and clutter stress. Acoustic views expose spectral cadence and node agreement. Passive-RF views expose no-signal, RFI burst, sparse cue, and provenance-quality geometry. Fusion views expose source confidence, stale-track penalties, cross-sensor confirmation, and false-track accounting.
+
+Accepted processing methods include CA-CFAR and OS-CFAR style thresholding, MTD concentration, M/N confirmation, tabular ML, sequence ML, and human-engineered layered fusion \cite{rohling1983,richards2014,kay1998,vantrees2002,fawcett2006,davis2006,saito2015,pedregosa2011}.
+
+\section{Sensor Fusion Before EI}
+Fusion is powerful because each branch sees a different failure mode. Radar can separate range-Doppler structure but is vulnerable to clutter, birds, multipath, and low-altitude transition. Acoustic cues add propulsion cadence but are weather/noise sensitive. Passive-RF provenance can help when signals exist but must also represent RF silence and RFI contamination.
+
